<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan de Velasco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1727–1792</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan de Velasco was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priest and historian born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. Exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with the Jesuits in 1767, he wrote there his Historia del Reino de Quito, completed in 1789, which described a pre-Inca Kingdom of Quito ruled by the Shyri kings of the Cara people. Modern scholars regard that account as largely legendary, but it shaped Ecuadorian national identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-velasco.jpg" alt="Juan de Velasco" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eladio Sevilla · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juandevelasco-pintura.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
